WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.480
"Pine Mouth Syndrome: Prolonged
Bitter Taste from Certain Pine Nuts "

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.520
The reason I make my pesto with walnuts
instead of the more traditional pine nuts

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.480
is not only because walnuts
are probably healthier —

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.520
we're talking 20 times
more polyphenols —

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.040
but also because of a mysterious
phenomenon known as PMS.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.440
Nope, not that PMS.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:34.816
Pine Mouth Syndrome, characterized
by what has become my favorite

00:00:34.816 --> 00:00:40.874
word of the week: cacogeusia,
meaning a bad taste in your mouth.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.520
You can get it from heavy metal toxicity,
seafood toxins, certain nutritional

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.440
and neurologic disorders, or from
eating the wrong kind of pine nuts.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.320
Termed "Pine Mouth" by the public,
a few days after eating pine nuts

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.440
you get this persistent metallic
or bitter taste in your mouth

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.480
that can last for weeks. Thousands
of cases have been reported.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:07.760
Raw versus cooked pine nuts
doesn't seem to matter.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.200
Could the cause be some
unidentified toxin present in some

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.360
varieties of non-edible pine nuts?
Out of more than 100 different kinds

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.240
of pine trees, the nuts of only about
30 are considered to be edible.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.600
So pine nut samples from stricken
consumers were analyzed,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.320
and indeed they all contained
nuts from Chinese white pine,

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.600
which is not reported to be edible. That
tree is typically only used for lumber.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.040
These are the good ones;
these are the bad ones.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.640
It's like a game: good,
bad, good, good.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.080
You don't know it's the Chinese
white pine nuts, though,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.000
until you put it to the test.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.240
Researchers had a few folks consume
six to eight Chinese white pine nuts.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.600
Most of the subjects hadn't ever
heard of Pine Mouth syndrome,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.480
and, boom, they all developed
symptoms. We still don't know

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.560
exactly what it is in those nuts
that causes such a weird reaction.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.160
We just know to stay away
from those kinds of pine nuts.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.160
So what kinds of pine nuts do
we have on our shelves here?

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.480
All kinds apparently, including
those associated with pine mouth.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.600
Good, good, good, bad, good.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:25.440
So, unsurprisingly, hundreds of cases
have been reported in the U.S. as well.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.160
Most of the implicated nuts
were reportedly labeled

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.080
as originating from Asia,
and in most cases China.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.240
Europe actually did something
about it and demanded China stop

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.400
sending them toxic nuts,
which they did in 2011.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.240
And this export restriction likely
resulted in less being imported

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:49.840
into the U.S. as well, given the
decline in cases going into 2012.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.480
Rare cases still happen,
though, as evidenced by

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.280
an active Facebook group
entitled "Damn You Pine Nuts."

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:00.320
The primary reason I made
this video is just to allay fears

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.080
should this ever happen to you.
Although there are no proven therapies,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.738
Pine Mouth syndrome appears to be
benign and just goes away on its own.

